AI Brief — Monday, 26 October 2026 (IST) — covering Sunday 25 October 2026

> Governments moved on AI's risks: the UK put £100m into a "world first" AI biosecurity lab and Pennsylvania made AI-made ads carry labels, while Block showed a voice model that sounds startlingly human and China's tech giants bid up pay for engineers who turn AI into products.

# WORLD

### UK sets up a £100m AI Biosecurity Lab, backed by OpenAI and Anthropic credits
The UK government says it will create the AI Biosecurity Lab, which it calls the world's first organisation dedicated to AI and biosecurity, with £100 million of public money announced ahead of the Budget. OpenAI and Anthropic have committed up to £200 million between them in compute, model credits, trusted model access and technical support. The lab will run as an independent non-profit and will first work on spotting biological threats earlier and speeding up vaccines and treatments. The BBC reports the Budget also adds £120 million for the UK's AI Security Institute. Full details of the lab's remit have not yet been published. (Themes: AI policy and regulation; AI breakthroughs)

Source: GOV.UK https://www.gov.uk/government/news/introducing-the-ai-biosecurity-lab
Also: BBC News https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cqdjv8xllvyyo

### Brazil votes in a runoff shaped by fights over deepfakes
Brazil held its presidential runoff on Sunday after a campaign in which the electoral court received a record 804 election-related filings, many over disinformation and AI-manipulated content, and banned deepfakes in campaign ads. Meta was also under scrutiny over how its platforms treated the two candidates. (Theme: AI policy and regulation)

Source: UPI https://www.upi.com/Top_News/World-News/2026/10/23/latam-brazil-presidential-runoff/9551792765244/

No new geopolitical or military AI incident with global reach was confirmed in this window.

# USA

### Pennsylvania will require labels on AI-generated ads
Governor Josh Shapiro signed the Artificial Intelligence Deceptive Advertising Act, making Pennsylvania one of the first states to require disclosures on ads that have been significantly modified or generated by AI, with the label in the same format as the ad. It applies to any ad shown in the state, wherever it was made. The Verge notes carve-outs for parody, satire, commentary and "evidently fictional" content, and says terms like "significantly modified" are left open to interpretation. (Theme: AI policy and regulation)

Source: The Verge https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1018187/pennsylvania-ai-deceptive-advertising-act

### Block's Dessa voice model laughs, pauses and talks over you like a person
Jack Dorsey's Block released Dessa, a full-duplex speech model that listens and speaks at the same time and was trained entirely on human speech rather than routed through text. Block says it can run on a consumer machine such as a MacBook M4 Max, but there is no public access yet, and the company has asked safety researchers to test it first. The "most authentic" claims are Block's own and have not been independently tested. (Themes: new AI models and tools; AI breakthroughs)

Source: TechCrunch https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/24/blocks-new-voice-model-sounds-eerily-human/

# CHINA

### China's tech giants offer close to 1 million yuan for engineers who turn AI into products
ByteDance, Tencent and Alibaba are stepping up hiring of forward-deployed engineers, who work directly with customers to build AI into their businesses, with annual packages approaching 1 million yuan (about US$150,000). Job postings for the role in China rose 1,523% in the first seven months of the year compared with a year earlier, according to the networking platform Maimai, making it the fastest-growing AI technical role in its data. (Themes: AI industry and startup news; technology trends)

Source: South China Morning Post https://www.scmp.com/tech/tech-trends/article/3371925/chinas-tech-giants-are-paying-1-million-yuan-new-kind-ai-engineer

### Chinese think tank calls for wider AI access for developing countries
CGTN reports a new report from the China Institute of International Studies urging broader access to AI for developing countries. (Theme: AI policy and regulation)

Source: CGTN https://news.cgtn.com/news/2026-10-25/CIIS-report-urges-wider-AI-access-for-developing-countries-1RwzGtokVxu/p.html

# ASIA

### South Korea starts designing a single defence AI data centre
South Korea's defence ministry has begun planning a Defence AI Data Centre that would pool GPUs now scattered across the army, navy, air force and agencies into one cloud-based resource, with a data hub for military data and a secure zone for working with classified data. The planning study is due to finish in the second half of next year, and the centre is expected to train defence-specific vision-language and physical-AI models and later support next-generation command and control. (Themes: technology trends; AI policy and regulation)

Source: Electronic Times (etnews, in Korean) https://www.etnews.com/20261023000175

# INDIA

### NCERT rolls out a 20-hour AI course for classes 9 to 12
NCERT released an activity-based module on computational thinking and AI for classes 9 to 12, built with IIT Madras and IIT Jodhpur. It covers how machines learn from examples, AI's limits, spotting deepfakes, data privacy and checking AI output rather than trusting it, and can be taught with or without digital devices. (Themes: AI policy and regulation; technology trends)

Source: The Hindu (PTI) https://www.thehindu.com/education/20-hour-introductory-module-on-computational-thinking-and-artificial-intelligence-released-by-ncert/article71622775.ece

No new Indian AI model, breakthrough or startup story could be verified in this window.

